{'status': 'success', 'count': 1, 'memories': [{'id': 'mem_001', 'type': 'knowledge', 'abstract': 'TopicState: Research directions in polymer deep learning and active hypotheses regarding interpretable physics-inspired neural network architectures. Currently restricted to existing polymer datasets, but leveraging validated hypotheses from other molecular deep learning tasks is permitted.', 'keyword': 'TopicState', 'content': {'research_goal': 'Accumulate knowledge and experience through continuous learning and practice to produce top-tier research in the field of polymer deep learning.', 'research_direction': 'With a total of 6 days available, dedicate the first half to property prediction tasks and the latter half to gradually attempting pre-training.\nUse Tg/Tm/Egc/PI1M as a sandbox, prioritizing physics-inspired deep learning models and testable mechanistic interpretability to gain general polymer deep learning insights and methodologies.\nEstablish fingerprint+LightGBM baseline before testing complex neural architectures - MLP+ECFP4 already achieves 38.78°C RMSE, beating reported GAT baseline.\nFocus algorithmic innovation on polymer-specific inductive biases validated in literature: periodicity (PerioGT), long-range interactions (LSR-MP), hierarchical decomposition (PolyConf).\nImplement architectures with validated polymer-specific inductive biases rather than generic neural architectures.\nAccept fingerprint dominance: ECFP4 provides 66% of model performance. Design lightweight GNN to complement, not replace, fingerprint features.', 'benchmark and datasets': ['Prioritize the Tg dataset to establish SOTA first, then generalize to Tm/Egc. Reserve PI1M strictly for pre-training and avoid premature usage.', 'Tg: 7170 one repeat unit p-SMILES and their corresponding Glass Transition Temperatures. Vanilla baselines: MLP+ECFP4=38.78, GCN=45.69. Reported baseline: GAT=50.5. SOTA: PerioGT=30.8.', 'Tm: 3651 one repeat unit p-SMILES and their corresponding Melting Points. Prediction task baseline: rmse=79.3 by GAT, SOTA: rmse=45.4 by PerioGT', 'Egc: 3380 one repeat unit p-SMILES and their corresponding Chain Band Gaps. Prediction task baseline: rmse=0.63 by GAT, SOTA: rmse=0.40 by PerioGT', 'PI1M: 1 million unlabeled one repeat unit p-SMILES.', 'Key finding: Fingerprint+tree models are strong baselines for small polymer datasets (<10K samples). ECFP4 fingerprints encode substructure information that simple GCN misses.'], 'active_hypotheses': 'These hypotheses carry uncertainty; as knowledge and experience accumulate, they should be continuously revised to improve the quality of the hypothesis space.\n\nData_Centric_Foundation [REJECTED]: Initial hypothesis that noise and lack of spatial context were primary bottlenecks. REJECTED with high confidence - dataset is clean, MLP+ECFP4 achieves 38.78 (vs GAT baseline 50.5), gap to SOTA (30.8) is algorithmic. Fingerprint features outperform simple graph structure.\n\nLong_Short_Range_Message_Passing [RANK 1 - ELEVATED]: Polymer physics governed by multi-scale interplay of local covalent chemistry and global chain entanglement. ELEVATED to Rank 1 after Day 3 PIPGA failure invalidated periodicity-as-dominant-mechanism hypothesis. STRENGTHENED by: (1) LSR-MP showing 40% improvement through explicit long-range modules, (2) Ewald MP paper (10-16% improvement via long-range electrostatics), (3) CELLI achieving SOTA through multi-scale cell representations, (4) polymer entanglement physics - chain segments 10-100+ atoms apart interact. Day 3 gap analysis: ~1-3 RMSE of the 8.19 RMSE gap may come from missing long-range interactions. Most actionable architecture for polymer Tg prediction.\n\nGraph_Transformer_for_Global_Topology [RANK 2 - WEAKENED]: Classical GNNs suffer from over-smoothing and fail to capture long-range entanglement. WEAKENED by Day 3 PIPGA negative result: Matformer-style periodic invariance does NOT transfer from 3D crystals to 1D polymers (multi-edge: +0.11 RMSE, self-loop: +0.95 RMSE - both negligible). PerioGT achieves SOTA (30.8) but mechanism remains unexplained - "periodicity-aware attention" is insufficient explanation. STATUS: Requires investigation of PerioGT\'s actual mechanism before recovery.\n\nSelf_Supervised_3D_Multimer_Pretraining [RANK 3 - CONDITIONAL]: Standard pre-training fails to capture polymer-specific spatial entanglement. CONDITIONAL on solving multimer assembly - naive concatenation fails (6-13% success). GPIP shows 10x data efficiency for self-supervised pretraining, but requires polymer-specific augmentations.\n\nDMPNN_for_Local_Valency [RANK 4 - DEPRIORITIZED]: DMPNNs pass messages along bonds for better local valency extraction. DEPRIORITIZED - Day 2 validated that ECFP4 fingerprints already capture local substructure information (+5.64 RMSE contribution). Day 3 confirmed fingerprint dominance (+25.72 RMSE, 66% of performance). Fingerprint integration supersedes bond-level message passing innovation.', 'daily_journal': [{'day': 1, 'journal': "## Day 1 Journal: 2026-03-19\n\n### Literature Review\nSelected and analyzed 4 papers: PolyConf (hierarchical polymer conformation generation), Open Polymer Challenge (distribution shifts in polymer prediction), LSR-MP (long-short range message passing for non-local interactions), and PerioGT (periodicity-aware graph transformer achieving SOTA on Tg/Tm/Egc). Key insight: fingerprint+tree models dominated the competition, and PerioGT's periodicity-aware attention achieves current SOTA.\n\n### Hypothesis Test: Data_Centric_Foundation\n**REJECTED with high confidence.** Executed the foundational hypothesis that data quality issues explain the baseline-to-SOTA gap. Results showed the Tg dataset is remarkably clean: 100% valid SMILES, zero duplicates, no statistical outliers. The 26% performance gap (MLP+ECFP4 at 38.78 RMSE vs PerioGT SOTA at 30.8) is due to algorithmic sophistication, not data artifacts.\n\n### Baselines Established\n- MLP with ECFP4 fingerprints: RMSE 38.78°C (R²=0.8781) — beats reported GAT baseline (50.5)\n- Simple GCN: RMSE 45.69°C (R²=0.8307)\n- Key finding: Fingerprint features encode substructure information that simple graph message passing misses\n\n### Multimer Generation Attempt\nFailed with 6-13% success rate using naive SMILES concatenation. Requires sophisticated polymer chain assembly algorithms (PolyConf-style hierarchical approach).\n\n### Hypothesis Updates\n- Data_Centric_Foundation: REJECTED\n- Graph_Transformer_for_Global_Topology: Elevated to Rank 1 (validated by PerioGT SOTA)\n- Long_Short_Range_Message_Passing: Elevated to Rank 2 (validated by LSR-MP 40% improvement)\n- Self_Supervised_3D_Multimer_Pretraining: Rank 3, conditional on solving multimer assembly\n- DMPNN_for_Local_Valency: Deprioritized to Rank 4\n\n### Ideas Generated for Day 2\nReasonable: (1) Fingerprint-Periodic Graph Fusion via attention, (2) Hierarchical Atom-RepeatUnit Message Passing adapted from LSR-MP. Concept-leaping: (1) Cyclic Contrastive Learning exploiting polymer repeat unit symmetry, (2) Polymer Band Structure using graph Laplacian eigenvalues as 1D crystal representation."}, {'day': 2, 'journal': '## Day 2 Journal: 2026-03-20\n\n### Literature Review\nSelected 4 papers aligned with Rank 1 hypothesis (Graph Transformer for Global Topology): Matformer (periodic invariance for crystals, 53% MAE degradation when broken), MoCL (domain knowledge augmentations for contrastive learning), GPIP (geometry-enhanced pretraining, 10× data efficiency), and SIMG (stereoelectronics-infused graphs with small-molecule-to-protein extrapolation). Key insight: Matformer provides theoretical foundation—periodicity encoding must be in attention mechanism design (periodic invariance), not graph topology modifications.\n\n### Experiment: FP-PGNN (Fingerprint-Periodic Graph Neural Network)\nExecuted the Day 1 idea of fusing ECFP4 fingerprints with periodic graph attention. Results:\n\n| Model | RMSE | Finding |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Full FP-PGNN | **37.04 ± 0.68** | Beats baseline |\n| No fingerprint | 42.67 ± 2.06 | Fingerprint: **+5.64 RMSE** |\n| No periodic edges | 37.33 ± 0.73 | Periodic: **+0.29 RMSE** |\n\n**Validated:** Fingerprint contribution is dominant (+5.64 RMSE, 19× larger than periodic edges), explaining why MLP+ECFP4 (38.78) vastly outperforms GCN (45.69).\n\n**Failed:** Simple virtual edges between head/tail atoms contribute minimally (+0.29 RMSE). Periodicity encoding belongs in attention weights (Matformer-style periodic invariance), not graph topology.\n\n### Hypothesis Updates\n- Graph_Transformer_for_Global_Topology: **REFINED** — periodicity in attention mechanism, not topology\n- Long_Short_Range_Message_Passing: **ELEVATED** — ~1-2 RMSE of gap may come from long-range interactions\n- Gap decomposition: ~4-5 RMSE from periodicity, ~1-2 from long-range\n\n### Ideas Generated for Day 3\n**Reasonable:** (1) PIPGA — Periodic-Invariant Polymer Graph Attention with Matformer-style edge-wise attention, (2) FMPA — Fingerprint-Modulated Periodic Attention. **Concept-leaping:** (1) TBSE — Tight-Binding Spectral Encoder (chemistry-aware Hamiltonian replaces chemically-blind Laplacian), (2) TIDE — Transfer Integral Distribution Encoder (physics-grounded chain rigidity → Tg).\n\n### Progress\nCurrent: 37.04 RMSE → SOTA: 30.8 RMSE (gap: 6.24). Estimated ~4-5 RMSE recoverable from proper periodicity encoding in attention.'}, {'day': 3, 'journal': "## Day 3 Journal: 2026-03-21\n\n### Literature Review\nSelected 4 papers aligned with Day 3 priorities: Matformer (periodic invariance for crystals, 53% MAE degradation when broken), Ewald-based Long-Range Message Passing (Fourier-space scheme, 10-16% improvement), CELLI (charge equilibration for electrostatics, SOTA on long-range systems), and PointGAT (graph attention + 3D geometry fusion). Key insight: Matformer's periodic invariance uses edge-wise attention without softmax and self-connecting edges encoding lattice geometry.\n\n### Experiment: PIPGA (Periodic-Invariant Polymer Graph Attention)\nExecuted Day 2 idea implementing Matformer-style periodic invariance for polymers. Results:\n\n| Model | RMSE | Finding |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Full PIPGA | **38.99 ± 2.10** | ❌ Target <34.0 FAILED |\n| No Fingerprint | 64.71 ± 4.45 | Fingerprint: **+25.72 RMSE** |\n| Softmax Attention | 41.47 ± 3.51 | Edge-wise: **+2.48 RMSE** |\n| No Self-Loop | 39.93 ± 1.79 | Self-loop: +0.95 RMSE |\n| Single Edge | 39.09 ± 2.14 | Multi-edge: +0.11 RMSE |\n\n**Critical Negative Result:** Matformer-style periodic invariance does NOT transfer from 3D crystals to 1D polymers. The core multi-edge mechanism contributed only +0.11 RMSE (negligible, within noise). Root cause: fundamental mismatch between 3D translational symmetry (crystals) and 1D linear chain periodicity (polymers).\n\n**Fingerprint Dominance Confirmed:** ECFP4 provides +25.72 RMSE contribution (66% of model performance). The GNN is essentially a weak enhancement to fingerprint-based prediction.\n\n### Hypothesis Updates\n- **Long_Short_Range_Message_Passing:** ELEVATED to RANK 1 — PIPGA failure invalidated periodicity-as-dominant-mechanism; LSR-MP has strongest evidential support (40% improvement, polymer entanglement physics)\n- **Graph_Transformer_for_Global_Topology:** DEMOTED to RANK 2 — Matformer adaptation failed; PerioGT mechanism remains unexplained\n- **Gap Decomposition Revised:** ~0.1-1.0 RMSE from periodicity (NOT 4-5 as estimated), ~1-3 RMSE from long-range, ~5-7 RMSE unknown (requires PerioGT investigation)\n\n### Ideas Generated for Day 4\n**Reasonable:** (1) LEMP — Long-range Entanglement-aware Message Passing with bipartite atom↔RU attention, (2) PAFT — Periodicity-Augmented Fine-tuning using contrastive learning on sliding-window RU views (addresses PerioGT's actual mechanism). **Concept-leaping:** (1) SCTE — Spectral Chain Topology Encoding (gap is representation problem, not architecture), (2) CRIP — Conformational Rigidity Intermediate Prediction (Tg determined by chain rigidity, not directly from structure).\n\n### Progress\nCurrent: 38.99 RMSE → SOTA: 30.8 RMSE (gap: 8.19). Matformer adaptation failed; pivoting to long-range interactions and PerioGT mechanism investigation. Fingerprint baseline (38.78) remains competitive with complex architectures."}], 'validated_findings': 'fingerprint_critical: ECFP4 fingerprints provide +5.64 RMSE contribution over pure graph (FP-PGNN ablation, Day 2). Day 3 confirmed fingerprint dominance: +25.72 RMSE (66% of model performance). Fingerprints capture substructures that GCN message passing misses. Effect is 19x larger than simple periodicity encoding.\n\nsimple_periodic_edges_fail: Virtual edges between head/tail atoms for polymer periodicity contribute only +0.29 RMSE (FP-PGNN, Day 2). Statistically insignificant. Periodicity cannot be encoded through simple graph topology modifications.\n\nmatformer_adaptation_failed: Matformer-style periodic invariance does NOT transfer from 3D crystals to 1D polymers. Day 3 PIPGA experiment: multi-edge mechanism +0.11 RMSE (negligible), self-loop mechanism +0.95 RMSE (small). Root cause: fundamental mismatch between 3D translational symmetry and 1D linear chain periodicity.\n\ngap_decomposition_revised: The 8.19 RMSE gap (PIPGA 38.99 vs PerioGT 30.8) decomposes into: ~0.1-1.0 RMSE from periodicity mechanisms (FAILED - not 4-5 as previously estimated), ~1-3 RMSE from long-range interactions (leading hypothesis), ~5-7 RMSE from unknown mechanism (requires PerioGT investigation).', 'day_3_priorities': '1. CRITICAL: Implement Matformer-style periodic invariance - proper graph construction for periodic boundary handling, edge-wise attention without softmax, self-connecting edges encoding repeat unit geometry.\n2. HIGH: Test attention-based fingerprint fusion (MoCL-style) vs simple concatenation.\n3. MEDIUM: Consider LSR-MP long-range module integration for polymer entanglement.\n4. Track: Current FP-PGNN 37.04 RMSE, Target SOTA 30.8 RMSE, Gap 6.24 RMSE (estimated 4-5 RMSE recoverable from periodicity encoding in attention).', 'day_4_priorities': '1. CRITICAL: Investigate PerioGT architecture in detail - understand actual mechanism behind SOTA performance. "Periodicity-aware attention" explanation is insufficient given PIPGA failure.\n2. HIGH: Implement LSR-MP style long-range message passing for polymer entanglement - currently the most promising mechanism with strongest evidential support.\n3. MEDIUM: Design lightweight GNN to complement fingerprints (not replace) - ECFP4 provides 66% of performance, focus on remaining gap.\n4. PIVOT: Accept fingerprint baseline (38.78), target SOTA 30.8 through long-range interactions + unknown mechanism discovery.\n5. Track: Current PIPGA 38.99 RMSE, Target SOTA 30.8 RMSE, Gap 8.19 RMSE (estimated ~1-3 RMSE from long-range, ~5-7 RMSE unknown).'}, 'connections': {}}]}

