s. Prediction task baseline: rmse=79.3 by GAT, SOTA: rmse=45.4 by PerioGT', 'Egc: 3380 one repeat unit p-SMILES and their corresponding Chain Band Gaps. Prediction task baseline: rmse=0.63 by GAT, SOTA: rmse=0.40 by PerioGT', 'PI1M: 1 million unlabeled one repeat unit p-SMILES.', 'Key finding: Fingerprint+tree models are strong baselines for small polymer datasets (<10K samples). ECFP4 fingerprints encode substructure information that simple GCN misses.'], 'active_hypotheses': 'These hypotheses carry uncertainty; as knowledge and experience accumulate, they should be continuously revised to improve the quality of the hypothesis space.\n\nData_Centric_Foundation [REJECTED]: Initial hypothesis that noise and lack of spatial context were primary bottlenecks. REJECTED with high confidence - dataset is clean, MLP+ECFP4 achieves 38.78 (vs GAT baseline 50.5), gap to SOTA (30.8) is algorithmic. Fingerprint features outperform simple graph structure.\n\nLong_Short_Range_Message_Passing [REJECTED - Day 4]: REJECTED for Tg prediction. Despite literature evidence from materials ML (LSR-MP 40% improvement, Ewald MP 10-16%, CELLI SOTA), LEMP experiment shows -0.08 RMSE contribution from long-range modules. CRITICAL INSIGHT: Tg is a LOCAL CHEMISTRY problem (functional groups, chain rigidity), NOT a spatial correlation problem like force fields. Literature on long-range interactions in MD simulations does NOT transfer to Tg property prediction. The physics of glass transition is fundamentally different from energy/force prediction.\n\nGraph_Transformer_for_Global_Topology [RANK 1 - WEAKENED]: Classical GNNs suffer from over-smoothing and fail to capture long-range entanglement. PerioGT achieves SOTA (30.8) but mechanism remains UNEXPLAINED. Both tested mechanisms FAILED: periodicity encoding (+0.11 RMSE, Day 3), long-range modules (-0.08 RMSE, Day 4). The term "periodicity-aware attention" is a MISNOMER - requires investigation of actual mechanism (data augmentation, contrastive learning, pre-training, or unknown attention design).\n\nSelf_Supervised_3D_Multimer_Pretraining [RANK 2 - CONDITIONAL]: Standard pre-training fails to capture polymer-specific spatial entanglement. CONDITIONAL on solving multimer assembly - naive concatenation fails (6-13% success). GPIP shows 10x data efficiency for self-supervised pretraining. May explain PerioGT\'s performance gap.\n\nDMPNN_for_Local_Valency [RANK 4 - DEPRIORITIZED]: DMPNNs pass messages along bonds for better local valency extraction. DEPRIORITIZED - Day 2 validated that ECFP4 fingerprints already capture local substructure information (+5.64 RMSE contribution). Day 3 confirmed fingerprint dominance (+25.72 RMSE, 66% of performance). Day 4 triangulated fingerprint dominance (+11.58 RMSE, 75% of performance). Fingerprint integration supersedes bond-level message passing innovation.\n\nINVESTIGATION_QUEUE: PerioGT\'s actual mechanism - data augmentation (SMILES permutation, repeat unit rotation), contrastive learning (MoCL-style domain knowledge), pre-training (GPIP-style geometry-enhanced), or unknown attention design.', 'daily_journal': [{'day': 2, 'journal': '## Day 2 Journal: 2026-03-20\n\n### Literature Review\nSelected 4 papers aligned with Rank 1 hypothesis (Graph Transformer for Global Topology): Matformer (periodic invariance for crystals, 53% MAE degradation when broken), MoCL (domain knowledge augmentations for contrastive learning), GPIP (geometry-enhanced pretraining, 10× data efficiency), and SIMG (stereoelectronics-infused graphs with small-molecule-to-protein extrapolation). Key insight: Matformer provides theoretical foundation—periodicity encoding must be in attention mechanism design (periodic invariance), not graph topology modifications.\n\n### Experiment: FP-PGNN (Fingerprint-Periodic Graph Neural Network)\nExecuted the Day 1 idea of fusing ECFP4 fingerprints with periodic graph attention. Results:\n\n| Model | RMSE | Finding |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Full FP-PGNN | **37.04 ± 0.68** | Beats baseline |\n| No fingerprint | 42.67 ± 2.06 | Fingerprint: **+5.64 RMSE** |\n| No periodic edges | 37.33 ± 0.73 | Periodic: **+0.29 RMSE** |\n\n**Validated:** Fingerprint contribution is dominant (+5.64 RMSE, 19× larger than periodic edges), explaining why MLP+ECFP4 (38.78) vastly outperforms GCN (45.69).\n\n**Failed:** Simple virtual edges between head/tail atoms contribute minimally (+0.29 RMSE). Periodicity encoding belongs in attention weights (Matformer-style periodic invariance), not graph topology.\n\n### Hypothesis Updates\n- Graph_Transformer_for_Global_Topology: **REFINED** — periodicity in attention mechanism, not topology\n- Long_Short_Range_Message_Passing: **ELEVATED** — ~1-2 RMSE of gap may come from long-range interactions\n- Gap decomposition: ~4-5 RMSE from periodicity, ~1-2 from long-range\n\n### Ideas Generated for Day 3\n**Reasonable:** (1) PIPGA — Periodic-Invariant Polymer Graph Attention with Matformer-style edge-wise attention, (2) FMPA — Fingerprint-Modulated Periodic Attention. **Concept-leaping:** (1) TBSE — Tight-Binding Spectral Encoder (chemistry-aware Hamiltonian replaces chemically-blind Laplacian), (2) TIDE — Transfer Integral Distribution Encoder (physics-grounded chain rigidity → Tg).\n\n### Progress\nCurrent: 37.04 RMSE → SOTA: 30.8 RMSE (gap: 6.24). Estimated ~4-5 RMSE recoverable from proper periodicity encoding in attention.'}, {'day': 3, 'journal': "## Day 3 Journal: 2026-03-21\n\n### Literature Review\nSelected 4 papers aligned with Day 3 priorities: Matformer (periodic invariance for crystals, 53% MAE degradation when broken), Ewald-based Long-Range Message Passing (Fourier-space scheme, 10-16% improvement), CELLI (charge equilibration for electrostatics, SOTA on long-range systems), and PointGAT (graph attention + 3D geometry fusion). Key insight: Matformer's periodic invariance uses edge-wise attention without softmax and self-connecting edges encoding lattice geometry.\n\n### Experiment: PIPGA (Periodic-Invariant Polymer Graph Attention)\nExecuted Day 2 idea implementing Matformer-style periodic invariance for polymers. Results:\n\n| Model | RMSE | Finding |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Full PIPGA | **38.99 ± 2.10** | ❌ Target <34.0 FAILED |\n| No Fingerprint | 64.71 ± 4.45 | Fingerprint: **+25.72 RMSE** |\n| Softmax Attention | 41.47 ± 3.51 | Edge-wise: **+2.48 RMSE** |\n| No Self-Loop | 39.93 ± 1.79 | Self-loop: +0.95 RMSE |\n| Single Edge | 39.09 ± 2.14 | Multi-edge: +0.11 RMSE |\n\n**Critical Negative Result:** Matformer-style periodic invariance does NOT transfer from 3D crystals to 1D polymers. The core multi-edge mechanism contributed only +0.11 RMSE (negligible, within noise). Root cause: fundamental mismatch between 3D translational symmetry (crystals) and 1D linear chain periodicity (polymers).\n\n**Fingerprint Dominance Confirmed:** ECFP4 provides +25.72 RMSE contribution (66% of model performance). The GNN is essentially a weak enhancement to fingerprint-based prediction.\n\n### Hypothesis Updates\n- **Long_Short_Range_Message_Passing:** ELEVATED to RANK 1 — PIPGA failure invalidated periodicity-as-dominant-mechanism; LSR-MP has strongest evidential support (40% improvement, polymer entanglement physics)\n- **Graph_Transformer_for_Global_Topology:** DEMOTED to RANK 2 — Matformer adaptation failed; PerioGT mechanism remains unexplained\n- **Gap Decomposition Revised:** ~0.1-1.0 RMSE from periodicity (NOT 4-5 as estimated), ~1-3 RMSE from long-range, ~5-7 RMSE unknown (requires PerioGT investigation)\n\n### Ideas Generated for Day 4\n**Reasonable:** (1) LEMP — Long-range Entanglement-aware Message Passing with bipartite atom↔RU attention, (2) PAFT — Periodicity-Augmented Fine-tuning using contrastive learning on sliding-window RU views (addresses PerioGT's actual mechanism). **Concept-leaping:** (1) SCTE — Spectral Chain Topology Encoding (gap is representation problem, not architecture), (2) CRIP — Conformational Rigidity Intermediate Prediction (Tg determined by chain rigidity, not directly from structure).\n\n### Progress\nCurrent: 38.99 RMSE → SOTA: 30.8 RMSE (gap: 8.19). Matformer adaptation failed; pivoting to long-range interactions and PerioGT mechanism investigation. Fingerprint baseline (38.78) remains competitive with complex architectures."}, {'day': 4, 'journal': "## Day 4 Journal: 2026-03-22\n\n### Literature Review\nSelected 4 papers: Periodic Polymer Graphs (polymer-specific graph representation, 20% error reduction), Ewald-based Long-Range MP (Fourier-space filtering, 10-16% improvement), Foundation ML Potential (polarizable long-range interactions), and SimPoly (polymer MLFF benchmark). Key insight: cutoff ~6.5Å necessary for polymer inter-chain interactions, validating long-range hypothesis for force fields.\n\n### Experiment: LEMP (Long-range Entanglement-aware Message Passing)\nExecuted the Rank 1 hypothesis that polymer Tg depends on long-range entanglement effects. Results:\n\n| Model | RMSE | Finding |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Full LEMP | **35.15** | ❌ Target <34.0 FAILED |\n| No Long-Range | 35.06 | Long-range: **-0.08 RMSE** (NEGATIVE) |\n| No Fingerprint | 46.73 | Fingerprint: **+11.58 RMSE** |\n\n**CRITICAL NEGATIVE RESULT:** Long-range message passing does NOT help Tg prediction. The voxel grid + structure factor + Ewald-style encoding contributed -0.08 RMSE (slightly negative). This REJECTS the Long_Short_Range_Message_Passing hypothesis.\n\n### Key Findings\n\n**Both Polymer-Specific Architectures REJECTED:**\n- Periodicity encoding (Day 3): +0.11 RMSE (negligible)\n- Long-range modules (Day 4): -0.08 RMSE (negative)\n\n**Fingerprint Dominance Triangulated:** Three independent experiments confirm ECFP4 provides 66-75% of performance: Day 2 (+5.64 RMSE), Day 3 (+25.72 RMSE), Day 4 (+11.58 RMSE).\n\n**Tg is a LOCAL CHEMISTRY Problem:** Glass transition temperature is determined by functional groups and chain rigidity, NOT by long-range spatial correlations or periodicity. Literature evidence from force field ML (LSR-MP, Ewald MP) does NOT transfer to Tg property prediction.\n\n### Hypothesis Updates\n- **Long_Short_Range_Message_Passing:** REJECTED for Tg prediction\n- **Graph_Transformer_for_Global_Topology:** Rank 1 but WEAKENED — PerioGT mechanism unknown\n- **INVESTIGATION_QUEUE:** PerioGT's actual mechanism (data augmentation, contrastive learning, pre-training)\n\n### Progress\nCurrent: 35.15 RMSE → SOTA: 30.8 RMSE (gap: 4.35 RMSE, UNEXPLAINED). Neither tested mechanism explains PerioGT's performance.\n\n### Ideas Generated for Day 5\n**Reasonable:** (1) SAPI — SMILES Augmentation for Polymer Invariance, (2) FGCL — Fingerprint-Guided Contrastive Learning. **Concept-leaping:** (1) PEL — Polymer Equivalence Learning (representation-first paradigm), (2) LFE — Learned Fingerprint Embedding (ECFP4 as bottleneck)."}], 'validated_findings': 'fingerprint_critical: ECFP4 fingerprints provide +5.64 RMSE contribution over pure graph (FP-PGNN ablation, Day 2). Day 3 confirmed fingerprint dominance: +25.72 RMSE (66% of model performance). Day 4 triangulated: +11.58 RMSE (75% of performance). Three independent experiments converge on fingerprint dominance. Fingerprints capture substructures that GCN message passing misses.\n\nsimple_periodic_edges_fail: Virtual edges between head/tail atoms for polymer periodicity contribute only +0.29 RMSE (FP-PGNN, Day 2). Statistically insignificant. Periodicity cannot be encoded through simple graph topology modifications.\n\nmatformer_adaptation_failed: Matformer-style periodic invariance does NOT transfer from 3D crystals to 1D polymers. Day 3 PIPGA experiment: multi-edge mechanism +0.11 RMSE (negligible), self-loop mechanism +0.95 RMSE (small). Root cause: fundamental mismatch between 3D translational symmetry and 1D linear chain periodicity.\n\nlong_range_rejected_for_Tg: Long-range message passing does NOT help Tg prediction. Day 4 LEMP experiment: -0.08 RMSE contribution (NEGATIVE). Tg is primarily determined by local chemistry (functional groups, chain rigidity), NOT spatial correlations. Literature evidence from force field ML (LSR-MP, Ewald MP, CELLI) does NOT transfer to Tg property prediction.\n\ngap_decomposition_final: The 4.35 RMSE gap (fingerprint baseline 38.78 vs PerioGT SOTA 30.8) decomposes into: ~0.1-1.0 RMSE from periodicity mechanisms (REJECTED), ~0 RMSE from long-range interactions (REJECTED, negative contribution), ~4.35 RMSE from UNKNOWN MECHANISM. Neither polymer-specific architecture tested explains PerioGT\'s performance. PerioGT\'s "periodicity-aware attention" is unvalidated - requires investigation of actual mechanism.', 'day_3_priorities': '1. CRITICAL: Implement Matformer-style periodic invariance - proper graph construction for periodic boundary handling, edge-wise attention without softmax, self-connecting edges encoding repeat unit geometry.\n2. HIGH: Test attention-based fingerprint fusion (MoCL-style) vs simple concatenation.\n3. MEDIUM: Consider LSR-MP long-range module integration for polymer entanglement.\n4. Track: Current FP-PGNN 37.04 RMSE, Target SOTA 30.8 RMSE, Gap 6.24 RMSE (estimated 4-5 RMSE recoverable from periodicity encoding in attention).', 'day_4_priorities': "1. CRITICAL: Investigate PerioGT's actual implementation - data augmentation, contrastive learning, pre-training, or attention design.\n2. STOP testing polymer-specific architectures - both periodicity and long-range hypotheses REJECTED.\n3. TEST simpler hypotheses: SMILES augmentation, MoCL-style contrastive learning, GPIP-style pre-training.\n4. ACCEPT fingerprint baseline (38.78 RMSE) and focus on complementary mechanisms.\n5. Track: Current fingerprint baseline 38.78 RMSE, Target SOTA 30.8 RMSE, Gap 4.35 RMSE (UNEXPLAINED)."}, 'connections': {}}]}

